# {{DISPLAY_NAME_MD}}

This starter is copied from Device Lab's checked-in minimal plugin template. It contains exact
identity detection, the six-field package manifest, and safe in-memory examples of partial
capability availability, cancellation, canonical input/output, diagnostics, and stop restoration.

- Manufacturer: `{{MANUFACTURER_MD}}`
- Baseboard: `{{BOARD_MD}}`
- SKU: `{{SYSTEM_SKU_MD}}`
- BIOS: `{{BIOS_MD}}`
- USB: `{{USB_MD}}`
- SDK API: `{{API_VERSION}}`

Replace the clearly named `example.*` capability and in-memory state in `DevicePlugin.cs` with
device-owned transports and services. Every write must snapshot the original value, validate current
bounds, read back the result, and restore temporary changes during deactivation. The generated
project references the exact SDK used by Device Lab: a source project when scaffolding inside the
WSGM checkout, or the shipped SDK assembly otherwise. Keep that explicit reference valid if the
scaffold is moved to another machine. The checked-in `example.*` implementation is hardware-free
teaching code. It is safe for local detection/lifecycle tests, but replace it with verified device
behavior before installation.

This plugin is your own work and you choose its licence. It links `WSGM.Device.Sdk`, which is MIT,
so nothing here obliges you to any particular one — a closed-source vendor plugin is fine. The
starter ships an MIT `LICENSE.txt` because that constrains you least; put your name in it, or
replace it outright. WSGM itself is GPL-3.0-or-later, but you are not linking WSGM.

Build, copy the produced DLL beside `plugin.wsgm.json`, then run:

```powershell
dotnet build {{ROOT_NAMESPACE}}.csproj -c Release -r win-x64
wsgm-device validate <package-directory>
wsgm-device test plugin <package-directory> --from <inventory.json>
wsgm-device pack <package-directory> --out <plugin.wsgmpkg>
```

The hardware action is separate and always attended:

```powershell
wsgm-device test hardware <package-directory> --from <inventory.json> --state-dir <new-directory> --action haptic
```

Use `--action controller`, or use
`--action capability --capability <id> --value <semantic-value> [--instance <id>]`, for the other
explicit attended actions.

After validation, close the WSGM shell. Installation makes this directory trusted
hardware code that may later inherit WSGM's elevation, so inspect the exact files first. Install or
replace the sole protected plugin slot with the installed WSGM maintenance command (it requests
elevation and refuses a machine-wide WSGM/Device Lab hardware owner):

```powershell
& "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\WSGM\bin\WSGM.exe" --install-device-plugin <package-directory>
if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { throw 'WSGM rejected the plugin installation; inspect wsgm.log.' }
```

The package directory must be separate from the protected installed slot. Pass exactly this command
and directory; maintenance flags cannot be combined with normal WSGM modes.

See `docs/device-plugin-authoring.md` in the WSGM repository for the complete workflow and safety
contract.
